        "1": {
          "evidence": "The 2011.0 x-position shows a red square marker with the printed label '37' nearby and a blue circle marker with the printed label '143' nearby. The red square is positioned higher on the page (vertically) than the blue circle.",
          "status": "supported"
        },
        "2": {
          "evidence": "The 2013.0 x-position shows a blue circle marker with the printed label '139' nearby and a red square marker with the printed label '32' nearby. The blue circle is positioned higher on the page than the red square.",
          "status": "supported"
        },
        "3": {
          "evidence": "At the 2012.0 x-position, there is a red square marker and a blue circle marker. Neither has a visible numerical data label printed next to it.",
          "status": "supported"
        }
      },
      "B": {
        "reading_checked": "If the printed numbers beside the colored marks are their countries' indicator values, then the 2011 and 2013 annotations show that screen height does not reliably rank the countries' numeric values; consequently, without a printed 2012 value or explicit shared-scale information, the 2012 comparison cannot be established from mark height alone.",
        "evidence": "The necessary condition for the first part of the reading is that the printed numbers are the indicator values. This is supported by the chart's data labels. The evidence for the claim that 'screen height does not reliably rank' is strong: at 2011, the red mark (37) is higher than the blue mark (143), despite 143 > 37. At 2013, the blue mark (139) is higher than the red mark (32), consistent with 139 > 32. This demonstrates that vertical position on the page is not a reliable proxy for the magnitude of the values because the scales are different (implied by the axis labels and the 2011 inversion). The second part of the reading states that without a printed 2012 value or explicit shared-scale information, the comparison cannot be established from height alone. The 2012 values are indeed not printed. The chart uses two separate y-axes (left for US, right for Canada) with no visible tick values, meaning the scales are not explicitly shared or comparable. Therefore, the conditions for the reading are met, and the conclusion follows from the visual evidence.",
        "status": "supported"
      }
    },
    "r2": {
      "O": {
        "1": {
          "evidence": "At the 2012.0 x-position, the red dashed-line square marker is positioned higher on the page than the blue solid-line circle marker.",
          "status": "supported"
        },
        "2": {
          "evidence": "The text 'Canada' is printed in red on the right margin of the plot, next to the right y-axis.",
          "status": "supported"
        },
        "3": {
          "evidence": "The text 'United States' is printed in blue on the left margin of the plot, next to the left y-axis.",
          "status": "supported"
        }
      },
      "B": {
        "reading_checked": "If the red dashed-square series is Canada and the blue solid-circle series is United States, and both use one shared vertical scale that increases upward, then a higher 2012 mark represents a larger 2012 indicator value.",
        "evidence": "The reading contains a critical condition: 'both use one shared vertical scale'. The visual evidence contradicts this condition. The chart has a left y-axis labeled 'United States' (blue) and a right y-axis labeled 'Canada' (red). This indicates two separate scales. Furthermore, the data labels (143 vs 37 at 2011) show that the scales are not shared; if they were, the blue mark (143) would be much higher than the red mark (37), but it is lower. Because the necessary condition of a 'shared vertical scale' is refuted by the chart's structure (dual axes) and data (inverted height vs value), the applicability of the reading's logic is refuted.",
        "status": "refuted"
      }
    }
  }
